AB  - Peer review is an incredibly important and revered process for establishing scientific rigor and facilitating effective discourse. However, many conventions of this process are ad-hoc and could benefit heavily from a more intentional design. I present our existing work applying fair allocation techniques to a major problem in peer review, the assignment of reviewers. We propose a simple but flexible algorithm for fair reviewer assignment, based on the classic Round Robin picking sequence. In addition, I discuss two other areas in peer review that are ripe for fair allocation and mechanism design, reviewer bidding and two-sided fair reviewer assignment.
