T2  - International Joint Conference on Autonomous Agents and Multiagent Systems Proceedings of the Third International Joint Conference on Autonomous Agents and Multiagent Systems - Volume 1
AB  - In liquid democracy, agents can either vote directly or delegate their vote to a different agent of their choice. This results in a power structure in which certain agents possess more voting weight than others. As a result, it opens up certain possibilities of vote manipulation, including control and bribery, that do not exist in standard voting scenarios of direct democracy. Here we formalize a certain kind of election control - in which an external agent may change certain delegation arcs - and study the computational complexity of the corresponding combinatorial problem.
