AB  - Budget aggregation deals with the social choice problem of distributing an exogenously given budget among a set of public projects, given agents' preferences. Taking a game-theoretic perspective, we study budget-aggregation games where each agent has virtual decision power over some fraction of the budget. We investigate the structure and show efficient computability of Nash equilibria for various common preference models in this setting. In particular, we show that equilibria for Leontief utilities can be found in polynomial time, solving an open problem from [15], and give an explicit polynomial-time algorithm for computing equilibria for ℓ1 preferences.
